Write the archive: NSArchiver produced ZERO BYTES for any object.

-[NSArchiver encodeRootObject:] WAS AN EMPTY METHOD, and every encode primitive under it,
encodeObject:, encodeValueOfObjCType:at:, encodeArrayOfObjCType:count:at:, encodeBytes:length:,
encodeValuesOfObjCTypes:, versionForClassName:, replaceObject:withObject:, data and archiverData,
was an NSLog stub with no body. So +archivedDataWithRootObject: answered an empty NSData for
anything at all, and every application in this port that archives an object into its defaults, a
file or the pasteboard stored nothing. Confirmed from both ends: Swift Publisher's own plist held
exactly one data value, FTUnlockedLayoutGuidelineColor, and it was 0 bytes.

This is the write half of the format NSUnarchiver.m already reads, and it is mirrored from that
file rather than invented: the NeXT typedstream, version 4. readHeader, readObject, _readObject,
readClass, decodeSharedString, decodeValueOfObjCType: and readType: are the methods to read beside
this one, and each has a counterpart here.

THREE THINGS THE DECODER DECIDES AND THE WRITER MUST OBEY:

  THE HEADER NAMES THE BYTE ORDER. readHeader takes "typedstream" to mean big-endian and
  "streamtyped" to mean little-endian, and swaps every multi-byte value when it disagrees with the
  host. Writing the one that matches this host lets everything else be written natively.

  THE LABEL ORDER IS THE FORMAT. Objects and classes draw from ONE counter, and an object takes its
  label BEFORE its class, because that is where _readObject: calls nextSharedObjectLabel. A class
  takes its label after its name and version and before its superclass. One step out of place and
  every back-reference in the archive names the wrong thing.

  A REFERENCE IS WRITTEN AS index + SmallestLabel, which is why SmallestLabel is -110: it puts
  every reference above the six special labels, so the reader can tell them apart. cider_writeInt
  therefore refuses to write a bare byte for anything below it.

The tables live behind the `ids` ivar rather than in new ivars, because NSArchiver.h is a public
header and an application may subclass NSArchiver: changing the ivar layout moves where a
subclass's own ivars land. `ids` is already a void * whose meaning is "encodeRootObject: has begun".

encodeConditionalObject: was also wrong in a way worth naming. It wrote the object
unconditionally, and a conditional reference exists precisely so that it does NOT: it is written
only if the object is already in the archive, which is how a back-pointer avoids keeping its target
alive. As it stood it turned every weak reference into a strong one.

MEASURED: darling-testsuite goes from 67 of 68 to 68 OF 68. The round-trip case exercises an
NSString, an NSNumber, an NSArray, an NSDictionary, and the same object stored twice, which must
come back as one object and does.

